{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MjWHxyyRsJEfne7c5xhNAy","type":"tool_result","content":"# Argo CD Security: Cluster Credentials and RBAC\n\n## External Cluster Credentials Storage\n\n\"To manage external clusters, Argo CD stores the credentials of the external cluster as a Kubernetes Secret in the argocd namespace.\"\n\nThe credentials include \"the K8s API bearer token associated with the `argocd-manager` ServiceAccount created during `argocd cluster add`, along with connection options to that API server.\"\n\n## Default Cluster Permissions\n\n\"By default, Argo CD uses a clusteradmin level role in order to: 1. watch & operate on cluster state 2. deploy resources to the cluster\"\n\n## Limiting Permissions\n\n\"Although Argo CD requires cluster-wide **_read_** privileges to resources in the managed cluster to function properly, it does not necessarily need full **_write_** privileges to the cluster.\"\n\nThe documentation suggests operators can \"modify such that write privileges are limited to only the namespaces and resources that you wish Argo CD to manage\" by editing the ClusterRole of the `argocd-manager-role`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"d6c119db-ffff-4c2c-9407-7c678ce31ac3","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:05:19.266Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":27053,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Argo CD Security: Cluster Credentials and RBAC\n\n## External Cluster Credentials Storage\n\n\"To manage external clusters, Argo CD stores the credentials of the external cluster as a Kubernetes Secret in the argocd namespace.\"\n\nThe credentials include \"the K8s API bearer token associated with the `argocd-manager` ServiceAccount created during `argocd cluster add`, along with connection options to that API server.\"\n\n## Default Cluster Permissions\n\n\"By default, Argo CD uses a clusteradmin level role in order to: 1. watch & operate on cluster state 2. deploy resources to the cluster\"\n\n## Limiting Permissions\n\n\"Although Argo CD requires cluster-wide **_read_** privileges to resources in the managed cluster to function properly, it does not necessarily need full **_write_** privileges to the cluster.\"\n\nThe documentation suggests operators can \"modify such that write privileges are limited to only the namespaces and resources that you wish Argo CD to manage\" by editing the ClusterRole of the `argocd-manager-role`.","durationMs":4134,"url":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/security/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JyeQXMzdPfYAzF7V4aTkuw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"opengitops.dev FAQ \"GitOps\" principles push pull agent\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"17 GitOps Best Practices to Improve Your Workflows\",\"url\":\"https://spacelift.io/blog/gitops-best-practices\"},{\"title\":\"Applying GitOps principles to your operations - The GitHub Blog\",\"url\":\"https://github.blog/enterprise-software/devops/applying-gitops-principles-to-your-operations/\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding GitOps Principles and Best Practices\",\"url\":\"https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-02-20-gitops-principles-guide/view\"},{\"title\":\"OpenGitOps 1.0 is finally here and why you should care | OpenGitOps\",\"url\":\"https://opengitops.dev/blog/1.0-announcement/\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the 4 Core GitOps Principles\",\"url\":\"https://akuity.io/blog/getting-into-gitops\"},{\"title\":\"GitOps Best Principles — OpenGitOps to Production — Babulal Tamang\",\"url\":\"https://babulal.com.np/blogs/gitops-principles.html\"},{\"title\":\"Home | OpenGitOps\",\"url\":\"https://opengitops.dev/\"},{\"title\":\"GitOps Explained — What It Is and Why It Matters\",\"url\":\"https://devtoolhub.com/gitops-explained/\"},{\"title\":\"DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/locnguyenpv/pullpush-in-devops-49pi\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about GitOps principles and the push/pull agent model:\n\n## OpenGitOps Principles\n\nThe OpenGitOps 1.0 Principles were developed by over 60 companies, 96 interested parties, and 34 co-authors, with each principle discussing the desired state of a system and how it should be operated.\n\nThe four core GitOps principles are:\n\n1. **Declarative**: A system managed by GitOps must have its desired state expressed declaratively.\n\n2. **Versioned and Immutable**: Desired state is stored in a way that enforces immutability, versioning and retains a complete version history.\n\n3. **Pulled Automatically**: Software agents automatically pull the desired state declarations from the source.\n\n4. **Continuously Reconciled**: Software agents continuously observe actual system state and attempt to apply the desired state.\n\n## Pull vs. Push Models\n\nThe key distinction in GitOps is the pull-based agent model rather than push-based CI/CD:\n\n- **Pull-based**: Instead of pushing changes into the cluster, an agent running inside the cluster pulls changes from Git.\n\n- **Push-based**: Push-based CI/CD is the traditional approach where a script running on your CI/CD server runs commands like kubectl apply to \"push\" deployments to your infrastructure.\n\nNote: The search results provided information about OpenGitOps principles from various sources referencing the official project, but did not return a direct link to an opengitops.dev FAQ page specifically.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"f574134c-4806-4e1a-aacb-94ca705e6c17","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:05:20.347Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"opengitops.dev FAQ \"GitOps\" principles push pull agent","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01UpkiEfuzN5tfyJBMEiiSKi","content":[{"title":"17 GitOps Best Practices to Improve Your Workflows","url":"https://spacelift.io/blog/gitops-best-practices"},{"title":"Applying GitOps principles to your operations - The GitHub Blog","url":"https://github.blog/enterprise-software/devops/applying-gitops-principles-to-your-operations/"},{"title":"Understanding GitOps Principles and Best Practices","url":"https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-02-20-gitops-principles-guide/view"},{"title":"OpenGitOps 1.0 is finally here and why you should care | OpenGitOps","url":"https://opengitops.dev/blog/1.0-announcement/"},{"title":"Understanding the 4 Core GitOps Principles","url":"https://akuity.io/blog/getting-into-gitops"},{"title":"GitOps Best Principles — OpenGitOps to Production — Babulal Tamang","url":"https://babulal.com.np/blogs/gitops-principles.html"},{"title":"Home | OpenGitOps","url":"https://opengitops.dev/"},{"title":"GitOps Explained — What It Is and Why It Matters","url":"https://devtoolhub.com/gitops-explained/"},{"title":"DEV Community","url":"https://dev.to/locnguyenpv/pullpush-in-devops-49pi"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about GitOps principles and the push/pull agent model:\n\n## OpenGitOps Principles\n\nThe OpenGitOps 1.0 Principles were developed by over 60 companies, 96 interested parties, and 34 co-authors, with each principle discussing the desired state of a system and how it should be operated.\n\nThe four core GitOps principles are:\n\n1. **Declarative**: A system managed by GitOps must have its desired state expressed declaratively.\n\n2. **Versioned and Immutable**: Desired state is stored in a way that enforces immutability, versioning and retains a complete version history.\n\n3. **Pulled Automatically**: Software agents automatically pull the desired state declarations from the source.\n\n4. **Continuously Reconciled**: Software agents continuously observe actual system state and attempt to apply the desired state.\n\n## Pull vs. Push Models\n\nThe key distinction in GitOps is the pull-based agent model rather than push-based CI/CD:\n\n- **Pull-based**: Instead of pushing changes into the cluster, an agent running inside the cluster pulls changes from Git.\n\n- **Push-based**: Push-based CI/CD is the traditional approach where a script running on your CI/CD server runs commands like kubectl apply to \"push\" deployments to your infrastructure.\n\nNote: The search results provided information about OpenGitOps principles from various sources referencing the official project, but did not return a direct link to an opengitops.dev FAQ page specifically."],"durationSeconds":7.326647799999992,"searchCount":1}}
