{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KFTQc8PpBvm8rJ884A7NoL","type":"tool_result","content":"# Applications\n\nThe Application CRD represents deployed application instances:\n\n\"The Application CRD is the Kubernetes resource object representing a deployed application instance in an environment.\"\n\nIt requires two key elements: \"source reference to the desired state in Git (repository, revision, path, environment)\" and \"destination reference to the target cluster and namespace.\"\n\n**Minimal Application Example:**\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Application\nmetadata:\n  name: guestbook\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  project: default\n  source:\n    repoURL: https://github.com/argoproj/argocd-example-apps.git\n    targetRevision: HEAD\n    path: guestbook\n  destination:\n    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n    namespace: guestbook\n```\n\n# Projects\n\nThe AppProject CRD provides \"a logical grouping of applications\" defined by three key components: \"sourceRepos reference to the repositories that applications within the project can pull manifests from,\" \"destinations reference to clusters and namespaces that applications within the project can deploy into,\" and \"roles list of entities with definitions of their access to resources within the project.\"\n\nProjects enable fine-grained access control, allowing administrators to restrict which repositories and deployment targets applications can utilize."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"f51a35d9-c8af-4577-8a74-9b8f902f3db3","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:05:35.926Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":83589,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Applications\n\nThe Application CRD represents deployed application instances:\n\n\"The Application CRD is the Kubernetes resource object representing a deployed application instance in an environment.\"\n\nIt requires two key elements: \"source reference to the desired state in Git (repository, revision, path, environment)\" and \"destination reference to the target cluster and namespace.\"\n\n**Minimal Application Example:**\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Application\nmetadata:\n  name: guestbook\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  project: default\n  source:\n    repoURL: https://github.com/argoproj/argocd-example-apps.git\n    targetRevision: HEAD\n    path: guestbook\n  destination:\n    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n    namespace: guestbook\n```\n\n# Projects\n\nThe AppProject CRD provides \"a logical grouping of applications\" defined by three key components: \"sourceRepos reference to the repositories that applications within the project can pull manifests from,\" \"destinations reference to clusters and namespaces that applications within the project can deploy into,\" and \"roles list of entities with definitions of their access to resources within the project.\"\n\nProjects enable fine-grained access control, allowing administrators to restrict which repositories and deployment targets applications can utilize.","durationMs":4913,"url":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/declarative-setup/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YA1GrfsnxBRJGmcYuzgiqT","type":"tool_result","content":"I've reviewed the provided web page content, but I cannot find any introductory prose that explicitly describes what \"bootstrap\" does in Flux—specifically that it installs Flux components, commits manifests to a Git repository, and configures Flux to manage itself.\n\nThe page is primarily a navigation index listing various bootstrap guides for different Git providers (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, AWS CodeCommit, Azure DevOps, Google Cloud Source, Oracle VBS, Gitea, and generic Git servers). While the page title is \"Flux bootstrap\" and sections reference \"How to bootstrap Flux with [various providers],\" there is no detailed introductory paragraph explaining the bootstrap process itself on this particular page.\n\nTo provide the exact quote you're requesting, I would need access to the actual bootstrap documentation pages (such as the GitHub, GitLab, or generic Git server bootstrap guides), which are linked but not included in the content you provided."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"07cfe37e-d46c-4346-8519-9b52cca6b3b4","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:05:44.116Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":159211,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"I've reviewed the provided web page content, but I cannot find any introductory prose that explicitly describes what \"bootstrap\" does in Flux—specifically that it installs Flux components, commits manifests to a Git repository, and configures Flux to manage itself.\n\nThe page is primarily a navigation index listing various bootstrap guides for different Git providers (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, AWS CodeCommit, Azure DevOps, Google Cloud Source, Oracle VBS, Gitea, and generic Git servers). While the page title is \"Flux bootstrap\" and sections reference \"How to bootstrap Flux with [various providers],\" there is no detailed introductory paragraph explaining the bootstrap process itself on this particular page.\n\nTo provide the exact quote you're requesting, I would need access to the actual bootstrap documentation pages (such as the GitHub, GitLab, or generic Git server bootstrap guides), which are linked but not included in the content you provided.","durationMs":10986,"url":"https://fluxcd.io/flux/installation/bootstrap/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012BnxoUExgqTAeWAFv7zJ6k","type":"tool_result","content":"# Flux Security Model: Authentication and RBAC\n\n## ServiceAccounts and RBAC Structure\n\n\"Flux installs a set of RBAC manifests. These include: A `crd-controller` `ClusterRole`, which: Has full access to all the Custom Resource Definitions defined by Flux controllers\"\n\nThe controllers use separate service accounts bound to different roles. Specifically: \"A `cluster-reconciler` `ClusterRoleBinding`: References `cluster-admin` `ClusterRole` Bound to service accounts for only `kustomize-controller` and `helm-controller`\"\n\n## Cluster-Admin Permissions\n\nOnly reconciler controllers have cluster-admin access: \"only [kustomize-controller and helm-controller] are the only two controllers that manage resources in the cluster\" and therefore bound to cluster-admin.\n\n## Impersonation and Multi-Tenancy\n\n\"In a soft multi-tenancy setup, Flux does not reconcile a tenant's repo under the `cluster-admin` role. Instead, you specify a different service account in your manifest, and the Flux controllers will use the Kubernetes Impersonation API under `cluster-admin` to impersonate that service account.\"\n\nThis allows policy restrictions to apply to the impersonated service account rather than cluster-admin directly."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"58a886af-6b0e-4d4e-b116-60fc206cdab0","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:05:45.205Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":174080,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Flux Security Model: Authentication and RBAC\n\n## ServiceAccounts and RBAC Structure\n\n\"Flux installs a set of RBAC manifests. These include: A `crd-controller` `ClusterRole`, which: Has full access to all the Custom Resource Definitions defined by Flux controllers\"\n\nThe controllers use separate service accounts bound to different roles. Specifically: \"A `cluster-reconciler` `ClusterRoleBinding`: References `cluster-admin` `ClusterRole` Bound to service accounts for only `kustomize-controller` and `helm-controller`\"\n\n## Cluster-Admin Permissions\n\nOnly reconciler controllers have cluster-admin access: \"only [kustomize-controller and helm-controller] are the only two controllers that manage resources in the cluster\" and therefore bound to cluster-admin.\n\n## Impersonation and Multi-Tenancy\n\n\"In a soft multi-tenancy setup, Flux does not reconcile a tenant's repo under the `cluster-admin` role. Instead, you specify a different service account in your manifest, and the Flux controllers will use the Kubernetes Impersonation API under `cluster-admin` to impersonate that service account.\"\n\nThis allows policy restrictions to apply to the impersonated service account rather than cluster-admin directly.","durationMs":10669,"url":"https://fluxcd.io/flux/security/"}}
