{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UGCw8msgsxZz1XyDxEz2L5","type":"tool_result","content":"# CNCF Glossary: Continuous Delivery Definition\n\n**What it is:**\n\"Continuous delivery, often abbreviated as CD, is a set of practices in which code changes are automatically deployed into an acceptance environment (or, in the case of continuous deployment, into production).\"\n\n**Distinction from Continuous Deployment:**\nThe glossary distinguishes the two by noting that CD \"includes procedures to ensure that software is adequately tested before deployment,\" whereas continuous deployment involves deploying directly to production. The entry clarifies that CD provides \"a way to rollback changes if deemed necessary,\" emphasizing the safety mechanisms that differentiate it from the automatic production deployment of continuous deployment."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"eecf7268-6c8a-4020-8611-a23636cccf0b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:06:28.970Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":48299,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CNCF Glossary: Continuous Delivery Definition\n\n**What it is:**\n\"Continuous delivery, often abbreviated as CD, is a set of practices in which code changes are automatically deployed into an acceptance environment (or, in the case of continuous deployment, into production).\"\n\n**Distinction from Continuous Deployment:**\nThe glossary distinguishes the two by noting that CD \"includes procedures to ensure that software is adequately tested before deployment,\" whereas continuous deployment involves deploying directly to production. The entry clarifies that CD provides \"a way to rollback changes if deemed necessary,\" emphasizing the safety mechanisms that differentiate it from the automatic production deployment of continuous deployment.","durationMs":2766,"url":"https://glossary.cncf.io/continuous-delivery/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SAQnioPriw8SQ99FAfC4sv","type":"tool_result","content":"# CNCF Glossary: Continuous Deployment\n\n**Definition:**\n\"Continuous deployment, often abbreviated as CD, goes a step further than continuous delivery by deploying finished software directly to production.\"\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\n\"Releasing new software versions can be a labor-intensive and error-prone process... Traditional software deployment models leave organizations in a vicious cycle where the process of releasing software fails to meet organizational needs around both stability and feature velocity.\"\n\n**How it helps:**\n\"By automating the release cycle and forcing organizations to release to production more frequently, CD does what CI did for development teams for operations teams... it forces operations teams to automate the painful and error-prone portions of production deployments, reducing overall risk.\"\n\n**Key distinction from continuous delivery:**\nContinuous deployment \"goes a step further than continuous delivery by deploying finished software directly to production,\" whereas continuous delivery stops at readiness for production release."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"88aa808e-0b3a-4f0d-b1d5-08cfc6db1585","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:06:30.136Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":49795,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CNCF Glossary: Continuous Deployment\n\n**Definition:**\n\"Continuous deployment, often abbreviated as CD, goes a step further than continuous delivery by deploying finished software directly to production.\"\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\n\"Releasing new software versions can be a labor-intensive and error-prone process... Traditional software deployment models leave organizations in a vicious cycle where the process of releasing software fails to meet organizational needs around both stability and feature velocity.\"\n\n**How it helps:**\n\"By automating the release cycle and forcing organizations to release to production more frequently, CD does what CI did for development teams for operations teams... it forces operations teams to automate the painful and error-prone portions of production deployments, reducing overall risk.\"\n\n**Key distinction from continuous delivery:**\nContinuous deployment \"goes a step further than continuous delivery by deploying finished software directly to production,\" whereas continuous delivery stops at readiness for production release.","durationMs":2777,"url":"https://glossary.cncf.io/continuous-deployment/"}}
