{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017iF5FiDxwmLqP82kBXqcX2","type":"tool_result","content":"# OpenGitOps 1.0 Key Points\n\n**What GitOps is:**\n\"GitOps is a set of principles for operating and managing software systems, derived from modern software operations.\"\n\n**Why principles are carefully worded:**\n\"The wording of each principle and linked glossary item was very carefully chosen.\"\n\n**Git not required specifically:**\n\"Likewise, many version control systems can be used in GitOps as long as they meet those two basic requirements and teams use them in a conformant manner.\"\n\n**Role of software agents pulling desired state:**\n\"Software agents automatically pull the desired state declarations from the source.\"\n\nAnd: \"The GitOps software agents have to be aware of the actual state of a system under management and attempt to apply the desired state.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"b79de787-8ff9-4588-8632-655f3792d5e6","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:07:05.421Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":151031,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# OpenGitOps 1.0 Key Points\n\n**What GitOps is:**\n\"GitOps is a set of principles for operating and managing software systems, derived from modern software operations.\"\n\n**Why principles are carefully worded:**\n\"The wording of each principle and linked glossary item was very carefully chosen.\"\n\n**Git not required specifically:**\n\"Likewise, many version control systems can be used in GitOps as long as they meet those two basic requirements and teams use them in a conformant manner.\"\n\n**Role of software agents pulling desired state:**\n\"Software agents automatically pull the desired state declarations from the source.\"\n\nAnd: \"The GitOps software agents have to be aware of the actual state of a system under management and attempt to apply the desired state.\"","durationMs":2404,"url":"https://opengitops.dev/blog/1.0-announcement/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016XnS5aMHS77pnhdxktYUU8","type":"tool_result","content":"# Flux Core Concepts - Key Definitions\n\n**GitOps Toolkit Definition:**\n\"In Flux, _GitOps Toolkit_ refers to a collection of specialized tools, Flux Controllers, composable APIs, and reusable Go packages available under the fluxcd GitHub organization.\"\n\n**Sources:**\n\"A _Source_ defines the origin of a repository containing the desired state of the system and the requirements to obtain it (e.g. credentials, version selectors).\"\n\n**Reconciliation:**\n\"_Reconciliation_ refers to ensuring that a given state (e.g. application running in the cluster, infrastructure) matches a desired state declaratively defined somewhere (e.g. a Git repository).\"\n\n**Kustomization Reconciliation Frequency:**\n\"The reconciliation runs every five minutes by default, but this can be changed with `.spec.interval`.\"\n\n**kubectl Changes Behavior:**\n\"If you make any changes to the cluster using `kubectl edit/patch/delete`, they will be promptly reverted.\"\n\n**Bootstrap:**\n\"The process of installing the Flux components in a GitOps manner is called a _bootstrap_. The manifests are applied to the cluster, a `GitRepository` and `Kustomization` are created for the Flux components, then the manifests are pushed to an existing Git repository (or a new one is created).\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"7d814f57-5f10-48c5-a9be-517d06240f4c","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:07:05.667Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":165905,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Flux Core Concepts - Key Definitions\n\n**GitOps Toolkit Definition:**\n\"In Flux, _GitOps Toolkit_ refers to a collection of specialized tools, Flux Controllers, composable APIs, and reusable Go packages available under the fluxcd GitHub organization.\"\n\n**Sources:**\n\"A _Source_ defines the origin of a repository containing the desired state of the system and the requirements to obtain it (e.g. credentials, version selectors).\"\n\n**Reconciliation:**\n\"_Reconciliation_ refers to ensuring that a given state (e.g. application running in the cluster, infrastructure) matches a desired state declaratively defined somewhere (e.g. a Git repository).\"\n\n**Kustomization Reconciliation Frequency:**\n\"The reconciliation runs every five minutes by default, but this can be changed with `.spec.interval`.\"\n\n**kubectl Changes Behavior:**\n\"If you make any changes to the cluster using `kubectl edit/patch/delete`, they will be promptly reverted.\"\n\n**Bootstrap:**\n\"The process of installing the Flux components in a GitOps manner is called a _bootstrap_. The manifests are applied to the cluster, a `GitRepository` and `Kustomization` are created for the Flux components, then the manifests are pushed to an existing Git repository (or a new one is created).\"","durationMs":4022,"url":"https://fluxcd.io/flux/concepts/"}}
