{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QBnimzRjrHkpVS2t7iRVFf","type":"tool_result","content":"# Factor III: Config - Key Excerpts\n\n**Definition of Config:**\n\"An app's config is everything that is likely to vary between deploys (staging, production, developer environments, etc).\"\n\n**Litmus Test:**\n\"A litmus test for whether an app has all config correctly factored out of the code is whether the codebase could be made open source at any moment, without compromising any credentials.\"\n\n**On Config Files in Repos:**\n\"This is a huge improvement over using constants which are checked into the code repo, but still has weaknesses: it's easy to mistakenly check in a config file to the repo.\"\n\n**Environment Variables Approach:**\n\"The twelve-factor app stores config in environment variables (often shortened to env vars or env). Env vars are easy to change between deploys without changing any code; unlike config files, there is little chance of them being checked into the code repo accidentally.\"\n\n**Grouping/Environments Issue:**\n\"This method does not scale cleanly: as more deploys of the app are created, new environment names are necessary, such as staging or qa.\"\n\n**Recommended Model:**\n\"In a twelve-factor app, env vars are granular controls, each fully orthogonal to other env vars. They are never grouped together as 'environments', but instead are independently managed for each deploy.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"591cae82-e4c3-4dd7-968a-7de2871b71ed","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:07:50.567Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":10058,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Factor III: Config - Key Excerpts\n\n**Definition of Config:**\n\"An app's config is everything that is likely to vary between deploys (staging, production, developer environments, etc).\"\n\n**Litmus Test:**\n\"A litmus test for whether an app has all config correctly factored out of the code is whether the codebase could be made open source at any moment, without compromising any credentials.\"\n\n**On Config Files in Repos:**\n\"This is a huge improvement over using constants which are checked into the code repo, but still has weaknesses: it's easy to mistakenly check in a config file to the repo.\"\n\n**Environment Variables Approach:**\n\"The twelve-factor app stores config in environment variables (often shortened to env vars or env). Env vars are easy to change between deploys without changing any code; unlike config files, there is little chance of them being checked into the code repo accidentally.\"\n\n**Grouping/Environments Issue:**\n\"This method does not scale cleanly: as more deploys of the app are created, new environment names are necessary, such as staging or qa.\"\n\n**Recommended Model:**\n\"In a twelve-factor app, env vars are granular controls, each fully orthogonal to other env vars. They are never grouped together as 'environments', but instead are independently managed for each deploy.\"","durationMs":5063,"url":"https://12factor.net/config"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GJWFdpobZHzNRXYthtZAGz","type":"tool_result","content":"# Factor XI: Logs Summary\n\n**On logs as event streams:**\n\"Logs are the stream of aggregated, time-ordered events collected from the output streams of all running processes and backing services.\"\n\n**On app responsibility:**\n\"A twelve-factor app never concerns itself with routing or storage of its output stream.\"\n\n**On output method:**\n\"Each running process writes its event stream, unbuffered, to `stdout`.\"\n\n**On execution environment handling:**\n\"In staging or production deploys, each process' stream will be captured by the execution environment, collated together with all other streams from the app, and routed to one or more final destinations for viewing and long-term archival.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"b9afe64a-c747-406b-86d4-73c21e4e5f25","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:07:50.816Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":8993,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Factor XI: Logs Summary\n\n**On logs as event streams:**\n\"Logs are the stream of aggregated, time-ordered events collected from the output streams of all running processes and backing services.\"\n\n**On app responsibility:**\n\"A twelve-factor app never concerns itself with routing or storage of its output stream.\"\n\n**On output method:**\n\"Each running process writes its event stream, unbuffered, to `stdout`.\"\n\n**On execution environment handling:**\n\"In staging or production deploys, each process' stream will be captured by the execution environment, collated together with all other streams from the app, and routed to one or more final destinations for viewing and long-term archival.\"","durationMs":2486,"url":"https://12factor.net/logs"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EX8kLuwrHAd5F5pBp1c9a9","type":"tool_result","content":"# Factor VI: Processes - Key Principles\n\n**Stateless and Share-Nothing Architecture:**\n\"Twelve-factor processes are stateless and share-nothing. Any data that needs to persist must be stored in a stateful backing service, typically a database.\"\n\n**On Sticky Sessions:**\n\"Sticky sessions are a violation of twelve-factor and should never be used or relied upon. Session state data is a good candidate for a datastore that offers time-expiration, such as Memcached or Redis.\"\n\nThe core principle emphasizes that applications should not rely on local memory or filesystem caching across requests, as multiple process instances may handle subsequent requests differently."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4f5a06-4bc3-4221-b900-b8461cc3e79d","uuid":"887cdc47-5b2b-4d10-800d-d6e8a447b890","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:07:51.810Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":9574,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Factor VI: Processes - Key Principles\n\n**Stateless and Share-Nothing Architecture:**\n\"Twelve-factor processes are stateless and share-nothing. Any data that needs to persist must be stored in a stateful backing service, typically a database.\"\n\n**On Sticky Sessions:**\n\"Sticky sessions are a violation of twelve-factor and should never be used or relied upon. Session state data is a good candidate for a datastore that offers time-expiration, such as Memcached or Redis.\"\n\nThe core principle emphasizes that applications should not rely on local memory or filesystem caching across requests, as multiple process instances may handle subsequent requests differently.","durationMs":2264,"url":"https://12factor.net/processes"}}
